WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Straight and kinked tails set how stiff a membrane is, and cholesterol buffers it.

2
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.860
Now watch a macrophage glide over a row of cells.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:14.300
It passes healthy cells by, then swallows a dying one before it bursts.

4
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:16.660
What is it reading?

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.800
The answer is not in the tails.

6
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:24.100
It is in the heads.

7
00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:27.200
A head group sets a lipid's charge.

8
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:33.220
Phosphatidylcholine carries no net charge, and it is enriched in the outer leaflet.

9
00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.000
Phosphatidylserine, or PS, is negative.

10
00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:39.880
It is held on the inner leaflet.

11
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:43.893
Held is the right word, because pumps keep it there.

12
00:00:45.273 --> 00:00:49.293
Flippases spend ATP to move particular lipids to the inner leaflet.

13
00:00:50.573 --> 00:00:53.773
Floppases also use ATP, and move lipids outward.

14
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:58.473
Scramblases, when switched on, let lipids cross both ways.

15
00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:01.633
In this healthy cell, they stay off.

16
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:08.073
So the inner face carries negative PS, and positively charged proteins in the

17
00:01:08.073 --> 00:01:09.493
cytoplasm meet it there.

18
00:01:10.913 --> 00:01:13.073
Phosphatidylinositol sits inside too.

19
00:01:14.513 --> 00:01:19.673
Phospholipase C cuts its phosphorylated form, PIP2, into two messengers.

20
00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:24.297
The outer face, the one the macrophage meets, looks different.

21
00:01:25.577 --> 00:01:28.157
It is rich in PC and in sphingomyelin.

22
00:01:29.597 --> 00:01:33.117
Glycolipids sit here too, and their sugars face outward only.

23
00:01:34.397 --> 00:01:36.237
Those sugars are the cell's name tag.

24
00:01:37.267 --> 00:01:38.767
Here is the one idea.

25
00:01:40.027 --> 00:01:41.807
A head group sets a lipid's charge.

26
00:01:43.187 --> 00:01:45.967
Pumps, spending ATP, set which face it sits on.

27
00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:50.107
Charge and location together set what the lipid does.

28
00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:53.807
Now this cell begins to die by apoptosis.

29
00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:59.167
Its flippases shut off, so PS is no longer pumped back inside.

30
00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:05.647
Scramblases switch on, and lipids start crossing both ways, erasing the difference

31
00:02:05.647 --> 00:02:06.647
between the faces.

32
00:02:07.867 --> 00:02:12.067
The macrophage glides back along the surface, past the healthy cells.

33
00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:15.727
What will it now find on the dying cell's outer face?

34
00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:24.233
Phosphatidylserine, now on the outside.

35
00:02:25.493 --> 00:02:28.793
The macrophage reads outer PS as an eat me signal.

36
00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:34.553
It engulfs the dying cell early, before the cell bursts and inflames the tissue.

37
00:02:35.853 --> 00:02:38.073
Its healthy neighbours keep their PS inside.

38
00:02:39.183 --> 00:02:42.183
So the flag was there all along, on the inner face.

39
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:45.323
Pumps spending ATP kept it there.

40
00:02:46.463 --> 00:02:48.243
A dying cell writes no new message.

41
00:02:49.543 --> 00:02:51.863
It simply moves an old one to the other face.
